WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:04.270
What’s the grossest thing that an actor
had to do to nail a performance?

00:00:04.270 --> 00:00:08.200
Well, one female actress had to actually put
a live bird in her mouth!

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:10.180
Keep watching to find out who it was.

00:00:11.180 --> 00:00:15.800
There has long been talk of Halle Berry going
to extraordinary lengths to get into character

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:21.880
as Vivian alongside Samuel L. Jackson in “Jungle
Fever,” Spike Lee's 1991 exploration of

00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:24.070
romance and race relations.

00:00:24.070 --> 00:00:28.340
Jackson portrayed the scene-stealing, drug-addled
brother of Wesley Snipes' lead character named

00:00:28.340 --> 00:00:31.560
Gator, while Berry was Gator's hardscrabble
main squeeze.

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:35.910
“Jungle Fever” features a memorable scene
that called for Jackson and Berry to bicker

00:00:35.910 --> 00:00:39.500
back and forth, when Gator wants some alone
time with his brother.

00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:44.310
For Berry, who came from the world of modeling,
it was an unexpected role, and one she had

00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:46.000
worked hard to land.

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:51.530
To prepare for the part, she spent several
weeks not bathing, with most reports claiming

00:00:51.530 --> 00:00:54.480
that she went unwashed for two weeks.

00:00:54.480 --> 00:01:00.280
Some even speculated that her avoidance of
soap and water went as long as two months!

00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:05.820
In 2012, the Oscar winner told talk show host
Wendy Williams that the Method tale was no

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:10.399
exaggeration, claiming to have avoided bathing
for about ten days.

00:01:10.399 --> 00:01:11.399
She said,

00:01:11.399 --> 00:01:15.649
"It's true [...] Ask Sam Jackson, he had to
get a whiff of it constantly!"

00:01:15.649 --> 00:01:18.469
Berry elaborated in a 2016 interview, saying,

00:01:18.469 --> 00:01:23.249
"Spike Lee wanted me to read for [a different
role] and I read that part fine enough, but

00:01:23.249 --> 00:01:27.860
then I said to Spike, 'You know, I really
am eyeing this crack ho role [...] It was

00:01:27.860 --> 00:01:29.789
an amazing way to start my career."

00:01:30.789 --> 00:01:35.100
Yes, the rumours are true: Nicholas Cage ate
cockroaches.

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:39.030
Cast in the role of Peter Loewv, a literary
agent suffering from paranoid delusions in

00:01:39.030 --> 00:01:44.310
the 1988 horror/comedy "Vampire's Kiss," Cage
was a fast-rising Hollywood star at the time.

00:01:44.310 --> 00:01:50.069
He was known for taking daring risks and making,
let's call them, “unique choices” in his

00:01:50.069 --> 00:01:51.069
mannerisms.

00:01:51.069 --> 00:01:56.100
In the years since, the box office flop has
become a cult hit, as well as a dependable

00:01:56.100 --> 00:01:57.600
Halloween watch.

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:02.659
"Kiss" also offers viewers the chance to watch
the actor do some off-the-wall things.

00:02:02.659 --> 00:02:07.689
From falling down stairs to tearing a room
apart to screaming the alphabet in its entirety

00:02:07.689 --> 00:02:12.950
— jumping on desks, chasing pigeons, and
pouring hot yogurt on his toes...

00:02:12.950 --> 00:02:18.391
The odd performance has since fed multiple
memes, and is considered a landmark in the

00:02:18.391 --> 00:02:24.250
cinematic evolution of Cage, perhaps the most
interesting actor of his generation.

00:02:24.250 --> 00:02:29.710
To convincingly portray Loew's spiraling descent
into insanity, Cage made a habit of offering

00:02:29.710 --> 00:02:33.780
to do more than what was being asked of him
— for one scene, in particular.

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:40.310
Though the script only called for Loew to
slurp down a raw egg, Cage felt more was needed.

00:02:40.310 --> 00:02:44.990
Speaking to “The Ringer” in 2019 about
the infamous movie moment, Bierman recalled

00:02:44.990 --> 00:02:46.240
that day on set.

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:47.240
He shared,

00:02:47.240 --> 00:02:51.430
"[Nicolas Cage] said to me, 'The thing I hate
most in the world are cockroaches [...] so

00:02:51.430 --> 00:02:53.140
let me eat a cockroach.'

00:02:53.140 --> 00:02:55.140
[...] I thought, 'This is terrific!'

00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:59.320
I sent my prop people down into the boiler
room [...] they brought me a box, divided

00:02:59.320 --> 00:03:01.540
up into little sections with tissue paper.

00:03:01.540 --> 00:03:04.550
The cockroaches were there, lined up for me
to cast."

00:03:04.550 --> 00:03:05.550
He added,

00:03:05.550 --> 00:03:10.170
"I think they're actually called water bugs
[...] They're bigger than cockroaches."

00:03:10.170 --> 00:03:13.540
“I couldn’t think of a more horrible job
if I wanted to.”

00:03:14.540 --> 00:03:18.110
Major spoilers for the 2014 murder mystery
thriller "Gone Girl."

00:03:18.110 --> 00:03:23.060
If you've seen the film, you'll know the gruesome
final act that Rosamund Pike must perform

00:03:23.060 --> 00:03:26.650
as Amy Dunne, jilted lover and missing person.

00:03:26.650 --> 00:03:30.430
After attempting to escape her apparently
abusive and unfaithful husband Nick — played

00:03:30.430 --> 00:03:35.180
by Ben Affleck — Amy hides out with another
formerly abusive ex boyfriend, Desi.

00:03:35.180 --> 00:03:39.680
She makes the world believe she has disappeared
while knowingly implicating Nick in the process.

00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:43.610
The film follows Nick's attempts to clear
his name and find his wife, with Amy being

00:03:43.610 --> 00:03:48.250
revealed as a ruthless, cunning woman out
to destroy her husband's life.

00:03:48.250 --> 00:03:53.290
In the film's climax, Amy decides to return
to Nick, and is forced to murder Desi and

00:03:53.290 --> 00:03:58.430
frame him for her disappearance so she and
her husband can live happily ever after.

00:03:58.430 --> 00:04:03.350
Actress Rosamund Pike was required to believably
slice the throat of actor Neil Patrick Harris,

00:04:03.350 --> 00:04:04.350
who played Desi.

00:04:04.350 --> 00:04:10.090
It was a key scene that she wanted to be prepared
for, so she did what any reasonable person

00:04:10.090 --> 00:04:11.090
would.

00:04:11.090 --> 00:04:14.200
She got a hold of some pig carcasses, and
pretended to murder them.

00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:16.160
The actress told Collider in 2016,

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:20.509
"If you're going to do something like that,
you have to do it with a certain degree of

00:04:20.509 --> 00:04:21.509
accuracy.

00:04:21.509 --> 00:04:25.370
I had no idea how much force you needed to
slice someone's throat.

00:04:25.370 --> 00:04:29.889
I actually went to a butcher and asked them
if they wouldn't mind me just using a box

00:04:29.889 --> 00:04:34.580
cutter on a pig carcass, just to understand
what it would be like."

00:04:35.580 --> 00:04:39.569
Matt Damon was nothing if not dedicated when
he took on the role of Max DeCosta, a blue

00:04:39.569 --> 00:04:44.630
collar worker in a dystopian future version
of Los Angeles in the 2013 film "Elysium."

00:04:44.630 --> 00:04:49.580
The movie was a much-anticipated follow-up
to director Neil Blomkamp's indie hit "District

00:04:49.580 --> 00:04:54.599
9," a science fiction drama featuring overt
commentary on South African apartheid.

00:04:54.599 --> 00:04:59.289
With "Elysium," Blomkamp took the formula
that worked so well before.

00:04:59.289 --> 00:05:04.419
This included its social commentary — and
dialed it up a notch: adding a bigger budget,

00:05:04.419 --> 00:05:08.550
bigger sets, and bigger stars like Damon and
Jodie Foster.

00:05:08.550 --> 00:05:13.990
But that expanded FX budget didn't mean the
entire movie would be filmed on greenscreen.

00:05:13.990 --> 00:05:18.569
So to portray Los Angeles in a future where
pollution, overpopulation, and climate change

00:05:18.569 --> 00:05:23.990
had ravaged the landscape, producers used
a garbage dump in Mexico City as a stand-in.

00:05:23.990 --> 00:05:28.930
At San Diego Comic Con in 2012, Damon spoke
to the curious crowd and told them about filming

00:05:28.930 --> 00:05:33.319
the sci-fi epic, and how the landfill location
was mostly fecal matter.

00:05:33.319 --> 00:05:38.529
"The helicopters would come through and we'd
be black with dust [...] Neil would come over

00:05:38.529 --> 00:05:43.160
with his mask and said, 'I promise you, the
photography looks great.'"

00:05:44.520 --> 00:05:49.919
Filming for the 2015 Alejandro Inarritu wilderness
epic "The Revenant" was notoriously difficult.

00:05:49.919 --> 00:05:54.499
The director himself called the production's
harsh conditions "a nightmare."

00:05:54.499 --> 00:05:58.740
Filmed on location in the Canadian Rockies,
the crew dealt with bitterly cold temperatures

00:05:58.740 --> 00:06:03.409
that made production difficult, and Inarritu
was heavily criticized for pushing his cast

00:06:03.409 --> 00:06:08.090
and crew too hard under harsh conditions,
in a shoot that went at least five months

00:06:08.090 --> 00:06:09.430
over schedule.

00:06:09.430 --> 00:06:14.590
Some crew members reportedly ranked it among
the worst experiences of their career; one

00:06:14.590 --> 00:06:19.199
cast member who didn't complain, however,
was lead Leonardo DiCaprio.

00:06:19.199 --> 00:06:23.960
To get into character and deliver his gut-wrenching
performance, DiCaprio — known for going

00:06:23.960 --> 00:06:28.819
above and beyond for his films — went to
extreme lengths that included sleeping in

00:06:28.819 --> 00:06:31.740
animal carcasses and eating raw bison liver.

00:06:31.740 --> 00:06:33.879
He shared in a 2015 interview,

00:06:33.879 --> 00:06:39.169
"Whether it's going in and out of frozen rivers,
or sleeping in animal carcasses, or what I

00:06:39.169 --> 00:06:40.169
ate on set.

00:06:40.169 --> 00:06:45.779
[I was] enduring freezing cold and possible
hypothermia constantly [...] I certainly don't

00:06:45.779 --> 00:06:48.320
eat raw bison liver on a regular basis.

00:06:48.320 --> 00:06:53.289
When you see the movie, you'll see my reaction
to it, because Alejandro kept it in.

00:06:53.289 --> 00:06:54.469
It says it all.

00:06:54.469 --> 00:06:56.599
It was an instinctive reaction."

00:06:56.599 --> 00:07:01.349
For the effort, Inarritu would become just
the third director to win back-to-back Academy

00:07:01.349 --> 00:07:06.050
Awards for Best Director, while DiCaprio would
take home his first Best Actor trophy.

00:07:07.050 --> 00:07:12.830
Based on the smash hit comic book by Todd
McFarlane, 1997's "Spawn" is infamously remembered

00:07:12.830 --> 00:07:16.979
today for its over-the-top, sometimes silly
tone.

00:07:16.979 --> 00:07:20.629
But the cast was certainly taking it seriously.

00:07:20.629 --> 00:07:25.099
John Leguizamo's character, for instance,
appears as a nauseating bulbous street punk

00:07:25.099 --> 00:07:30.360
in bizarre facial makeup, whose disgust for
humans is rivaled only by an appetite for

00:07:30.360 --> 00:07:32.610
all things horrifying.

00:07:32.610 --> 00:07:37.360
In one scene, the character eats a pizza covered
with live maggots.

00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:43.219
Like some other instances above, this immediately
led to a debate among viewers about how real

00:07:43.219 --> 00:07:45.069
the scene actually was.

00:07:45.069 --> 00:07:50.539
Eventually the conversation got so loud that
the actor himself had to respond.

00:07:50.539 --> 00:07:52.139
He tweeted in 2014,

00:07:52.139 --> 00:07:56.610
"Yes I did eat maggots in 'Spawn’ [...] But
[I] only swallowed a few!"

00:07:56.610 --> 00:07:59.529
He followed it up by warning his fans not
to try it at home.

00:07:59.529 --> 00:08:00.529
He said,

00:08:00.529 --> 00:08:01.529
"Ha ha.

00:08:01.529 --> 00:08:02.529
Don't go there!"

00:08:03.529 --> 00:08:07.389
Shia LaBeouf has earned the reputation of
a Hollywood eccentric, and in the right hands

00:08:07.389 --> 00:08:11.479
this can sometimes translate to powerful on-screen
work.

00:08:11.479 --> 00:08:16.259
This was the case when his talents were tested
in David Ayer's acclaimed 2014 World War II

00:08:16.259 --> 00:08:17.580
picture "Fury."

00:08:17.580 --> 00:08:22.210
Along with much of the cast, LaBeouf took
part in several weeks of military boot camp

00:08:22.210 --> 00:08:24.800
alongside a group of Navy Seals.

00:08:24.800 --> 00:08:25.800
Star Brad Pitt said,

00:08:25.800 --> 00:08:32.169
"It was set up to break us down, to keep us
cold, to keep us exhausted, to make us miserable,

00:08:32.169 --> 00:08:35.720
to keep us wet, make us eat cold food."

00:08:35.720 --> 00:08:41.180
But LaBeouf, who played a tank driver in the
film, took his Method performance one step

00:08:41.180 --> 00:08:45.400
further — going so far as to having one
of his teeth removed.

00:08:45.400 --> 00:08:49.970
He explained on "Jimmy Kimmel Live" that it
wasn’t as easy as one might think, saying,

00:08:49.970 --> 00:08:53.900
"It's not like you can go to some dentist
and go in there, like: 'Hey, I wanna get this

00:08:53.900 --> 00:08:55.120
tooth taken out'.

00:08:55.120 --> 00:08:57.470
They were like: 'You wanna do what?

00:08:57.470 --> 00:08:59.520
That doesn't make any medical sense at all."

00:08:59.520 --> 00:09:04.100
But ultimately, LaBeouf insists that he got
it done because he wanted to add some depth

00:09:04.100 --> 00:09:05.100
to the character.

00:09:05.100 --> 00:09:06.100
He added,

00:09:06.100 --> 00:09:08.090
"I found a guy in Reseda next to a Radio Shack.

00:09:08.090 --> 00:09:10.540
He didn't ask too many questions."

00:09:10.540 --> 00:09:15.500
To further Method this madness, "Fury" co-star
Logan Lerman told an interviewer that the

00:09:15.500 --> 00:09:19.440
scar on LaBeouf's cheek in the film was not
only real, but self-inflicted.

00:09:19.440 --> 00:09:20.440
Lerman explained,

00:09:20.440 --> 00:09:24.880
"They were putting cuts on Shia and I said,
'Yeah, yeah, it looks good' [...] And Shia

00:09:24.880 --> 00:09:29.490
was like 'No, it doesn't look real' ...[so]
he walks out into the hallway and says, 'Hey

00:09:29.490 --> 00:09:30.770
man, wanna see something fun?

00:09:30.770 --> 00:09:31.770
Check this out.'

00:09:31.770 --> 00:09:36.340
And he takes out a knife and cuts his face
[...] For the whole movie he kept opening

00:09:36.340 --> 00:09:38.170
these cuts on his face.

00:09:38.170 --> 00:09:39.500
That's all real."

00:09:40.600 --> 00:09:44.340
Among the most intensely-dedicated actors
in Hollywood today, Christian Bale always

00:09:44.340 --> 00:09:46.140
finds a way to stand out.

00:09:46.140 --> 00:09:51.660
He notoriously lost 60 pounds for the 2004
flick "The Machinist," then immediately bulked

00:09:51.660 --> 00:09:53.010
up to play Batman.

00:09:53.010 --> 00:09:59.270
For the Werner Herzog Vietnam war drama "Rescue
Dawn," Bale combined these methods and more.

00:09:59.270 --> 00:10:03.450
Taking on the role of real life war hero and
U.S. Navy pilot Dieter Dengler, Bale committed

00:10:03.450 --> 00:10:08.300
himself fully to embodying the part of the
long-suffering hostage of the Pathet Lao.

00:10:08.300 --> 00:10:12.990
Tortured and starved, the movie portrays Dengler's
struggle to endure his captivity, staying

00:10:12.990 --> 00:10:16.170
alive for his ultimate rescue by American
forces.

00:10:16.170 --> 00:10:21.990
Bale once again put his heart and soul into
a part, challenging himself both physically

00:10:21.990 --> 00:10:24.060
and mentally during the shoot.

00:10:24.060 --> 00:10:29.330
These adversities included wrestling a real
snake, losing more than sixty pounds, and

00:10:29.330 --> 00:10:32.780
being physically abused as part of several
torture scenes.

00:10:32.780 --> 00:10:39.240
But perhaps most nauseating, Bale ate live
maggots in an attempt to portray the depths

00:10:39.240 --> 00:10:43.590
of desperation his real-life character had
reached during imprisonment.

00:10:43.590 --> 00:10:45.440
He said in a 2007 interview

00:10:45.440 --> 00:10:47.680
"Oh, yeah, those were real maggots.

00:10:47.680 --> 00:10:51.140
They were very real [...] I didn't mind eating
the maggots."

00:10:51.140 --> 00:10:56.280
Asked what he wouldn't do, the actor remarked
that being set ablaze is something that crosses

00:10:56.280 --> 00:10:57.280
the line.

00:10:57.280 --> 00:11:01.950
"Look, I've done other things where people
have to be set on fire and jump three stories.

00:11:01.950 --> 00:11:04.700
I'm not doing that [...] I've got limits."

00:11:04.700 --> 00:11:07.320
“What’s the difference between you and
me?”

00:11:07.320 --> 00:11:10.070
“I’m not wearing hockey pads.”

00:11:11.180 --> 00:11:15.450
Veteran actor Tony Todd has had multiple iconic
supporting roles over the years, in movies

00:11:15.450 --> 00:11:18.220
like "Platoon" and "Night of the Living Dead."

00:11:18.220 --> 00:11:23.910
But it's his part as the titular horror villain
in the 1992 film "Candyman" that has become

00:11:23.910 --> 00:11:25.430
his calling card.

00:11:25.430 --> 00:11:31.370
His booming voice terrified moviegoers in
multiple films, and has haunted dreams for

00:11:31.370 --> 00:11:33.350
nearly thirty years.

00:11:33.350 --> 00:11:35.590
“You’re mine now.”

00:11:35.590 --> 00:11:41.130
But it was more than Todd's imposing deep
voice, towering physical presence, and razor

00:11:41.130 --> 00:11:43.340
sharp hook that scared viewers.

00:11:43.340 --> 00:11:48.140
The franchise has depicted swarms of bees
crawling out of him — from his sleeves,

00:11:48.140 --> 00:11:54.400
his chest, and even his mouth — and keep
in mind that in the early '90s CGI was a costly,

00:11:54.400 --> 00:11:56.590
rarely-utilized option for horror films.

00:11:56.590 --> 00:12:03.190
As a result, swarms of real bees were used
on set, and actor Tony Todd endured it all,

00:12:03.190 --> 00:12:06.550
receiving more than 20 bee stings during the
shoot.

00:12:06.550 --> 00:12:09.160
But don't feel too bad for the thespian.

00:12:09.160 --> 00:12:13.710
He made a smart deal with the film's producers
that ended up putting some good money in his

00:12:13.710 --> 00:12:16.240
pocket, alongside all that calamine lotion.

00:12:16.240 --> 00:12:19.530
He told Entertainment Weekly while looking
back on the film in 2019,

00:12:19.530 --> 00:12:24.640
"I negotiated a bonus of $1,000 for every
sting during the bee scene [...] And I got

00:12:24.640 --> 00:12:26.820
stung 23 times.

00:12:26.820 --> 00:12:31.380
Everything that's worth making has to involve
some sort of pain.

00:12:31.380 --> 00:12:35.760
Once I realized it was an important part of
who Candyman was, I embraced it.

00:12:35.760 --> 00:12:37.810
It was like putting on a beautiful coat."

00:12:38.810 --> 00:12:44.300
The 2012 movie "The Paperboy" didn't make
many waves with critics or at the box office,

00:12:44.300 --> 00:12:49.220
but at the time of its release the film was
nonetheless discussed, albeit for all the

00:12:49.220 --> 00:12:50.470
wrong reasons.

00:12:50.470 --> 00:12:55.010
Set in 1969, "Paperboy" tells the tale of
a reporter who goes back to his childhood

00:12:55.010 --> 00:12:59.030
hometown to prove the innocence of a death
row inmate found guilty of murdering the local

00:12:59.030 --> 00:13:00.030
sheriff.

00:13:00.030 --> 00:13:04.480
Matthew McConaughey plays the intrepid reporter,
but a tawdry secondary story involves the

00:13:04.480 --> 00:13:05.670
film's other two leads.

00:13:05.670 --> 00:13:10.360
Zac Efron plays his younger brother, Jack,
and Nicole Kidman plays an out-of-towner named

00:13:10.360 --> 00:13:13.070
Charlotte Hess, girlfriend of the accused.

00:13:13.070 --> 00:13:18.170
One scene in the film has Efron's Jack Ward
getting badly injured and stung by a group

00:13:18.170 --> 00:13:19.620
of jellyfish.

00:13:19.620 --> 00:13:24.620
As pop culture of the last few decades would
have you believe, there's only one way to

00:13:24.620 --> 00:13:30.230
overcome such pain, and that's by having someone
give you an impromptu golden shower.

00:13:30.230 --> 00:13:35.420
According to the Cleveland Clinic, this is
indeed just a myth, so please don't pee on

00:13:35.420 --> 00:13:38.610
friends and family without first consulting
a doctor.

00:13:38.610 --> 00:13:42.540
For the scene in question, Kidman actually
urinated on Zac Efron.

00:13:42.540 --> 00:13:44.240
Daniels said in a 2012 interview,

00:13:44.240 --> 00:13:48.671
"We just went for it and never thought twice
about it, because it made sense for the film

00:13:48.671 --> 00:13:50.440
[...] It was what it was."

00:13:50.440 --> 00:13:55.790
As the flick made its way through post production,
Daniels began getting doubts — but Oscar

00:13:55.790 --> 00:13:59.070
winner Kidman encouraged him to stick by his
instincts.

00:13:59.070 --> 00:14:00.070
Daniels recalled,

00:14:00.070 --> 00:14:04.050
"I think that I became more nervous about
it in the edit room, and I thought, I'm not

00:14:04.050 --> 00:14:06.370
actually going to show this, right?

00:14:06.370 --> 00:14:07.649
Is it vulgar?

00:14:07.649 --> 00:14:12.060
[...] And I called Nicole and said, 'I don't
know,' and she said, 'Lee, you made me pee

00:14:12.060 --> 00:14:13.230
on Zac Efron.

00:14:13.230 --> 00:14:16.170
If you don't put it in the movie, you need
to man up."

00:14:16.170 --> 00:14:18.970
And I was like, "All right."

00:14:19.970 --> 00:14:23.880
Joaquin Phoenix is known for his eccentric
ways, throwing himself into films like "Joker"

00:14:23.880 --> 00:14:24.930
and "I'm Still Here."

00:14:24.930 --> 00:14:29.410
For Paul Thomas Anderson's 2012 film that
flirted with the origins of Scientology and

00:14:29.410 --> 00:14:33.970
the struggle between faith and free will,
however, Phoenix may have gone to his most

00:14:33.970 --> 00:14:35.510
extreme.

00:14:35.510 --> 00:14:39.460
Portraying Freddie Quell, a veteran of the
second world war suffering from post traumatic

00:14:39.460 --> 00:14:42.850
stress disorder, Phoenix got very Method.

00:14:42.850 --> 00:14:47.660
So much so that he even wired parts of his
jaw shut so he could achieve the character's

00:14:47.660 --> 00:14:49.280
awkward speech patterns.

00:14:49.280 --> 00:14:54.540
It's a move somewhat reminiscent of how Method
hero Marlon Brando used cotton balls to land

00:14:54.540 --> 00:14:59.980
the role of Don Corleone, but it also made
for a wholly-unique performance in The Master—

00:14:59.980 --> 00:15:02.470
one that landed Phoenix an Oscar nomination.

00:15:02.470 --> 00:15:04.760
Phoenix explained the story in 2014, saying,

00:15:04.760 --> 00:15:07.780
"My dad sometimes would talk out of the side.

00:15:07.780 --> 00:15:10.420
He'd clench down one side of his mouth.

00:15:10.420 --> 00:15:14.990
And I just thought it represented tension
in this way, somebody that's just blocked

00:15:14.990 --> 00:15:19.960
and tight [...] So I actually went to my dentist,
and I had them fasten these metal brackets

00:15:19.960 --> 00:15:23.442
to my teeth on the top and the bottom and
then I wrapped rubber bands around it to force

00:15:23.442 --> 00:15:25.420
my jaw shut on one side.”

00:15:25.420 --> 00:15:27.140
Phoenix went on to explain,

00:15:27.140 --> 00:15:31.130
[...] After a couple weeks, the bands, they
weren't really strong enough to kind of hold

00:15:31.130 --> 00:15:34.840
it so I ended up getting rid of the rubber
bands and I still had these metal brackets

00:15:34.840 --> 00:15:38.450
in and so it made me constantly aware of my
cheek.

00:15:38.450 --> 00:15:42.590
You know, they had these pointy tips so they'd
tear up the cheek a little bit."

00:15:43.590 --> 00:15:48.420
Tim Burton's "Batman Returns" had some unsettling
moments, many of them belonging to Danny DeVito's

00:15:48.420 --> 00:15:49.420
Penguin.

00:15:49.420 --> 00:15:53.610
His grotesque appearance and disgusting eating
habits made it a bit unpleasant if you were

00:15:53.610 --> 00:15:55.970
attempting to chow down on a tub of popcorn.

00:15:55.970 --> 00:16:01.090
But it was the eating habits of the other
villain that makes this list, in particular

00:16:01.090 --> 00:16:04.390
an infamous scene involving a little birdie.

00:16:04.390 --> 00:16:08.590
During the scene in question, the villainous
Catwoman puts a live bird in her mouth as

00:16:08.590 --> 00:16:10.860
a way of threatening DeVito's Penguin.

00:16:10.860 --> 00:16:14.720
Actress Michelle Pfeiffer, who had beat out
the likes of Sean Young and Madonna for the

00:16:14.720 --> 00:16:19.950
in-demand part, was determined to do more
than simply toss a rubber prop into her mouth

00:16:19.950 --> 00:16:21.320
and call it a day.

00:16:21.320 --> 00:16:26.830
Instead, the actress wanted the full effect
— particularly the bird fleeing the coop

00:16:26.830 --> 00:16:32.200
when she opened her mouth — and actually
put a real, live bird in her mouth for the

00:16:32.200 --> 00:16:33.200
scene.

00:16:33.200 --> 00:16:34.200
“Nice birdie.”

00:16:34.200 --> 00:16:39.590
These days, after special effects and CG have
spent decades rendering audiences accustomed

00:16:39.590 --> 00:16:43.721
to cinematic trickery, you might not appreciate
the lengths Pfeiffer went to for the scene

00:16:43.721 --> 00:16:45.750
unless you know the backstory.

00:16:45.750 --> 00:16:48.030
Burton told “The Hollywood Reporter” in
2017,

00:16:48.030 --> 00:16:53.310
"I don't think I've ever been so impressed
[...] She had a live bird in her mouth while

00:16:53.310 --> 00:16:54.740
the camera was rolling.

00:16:54.740 --> 00:16:58.690
It was four or five seconds, and then she
let it fly out.

00:16:58.690 --> 00:17:01.520
It was before CG, it was before digital.

00:17:01.520 --> 00:17:04.959
It was so quick, it seems like it was an effect."

00:17:04.959 --> 00:17:06.260
Pfeiffer said of the scene,

00:17:06.260 --> 00:17:09.390
"I look back and say, 'What was I thinking?’

00:17:09.390 --> 00:17:13.459
[...] I could've gotten a disease or something
from having a live bird in my mouth [...] it

00:17:13.459 --> 00:17:15.130
seemed fine at the time.

00:17:15.130 --> 00:17:18.280
I don't think the bird was drugged or anything.

00:17:18.280 --> 00:17:21.209
We did that scene in one take."

